{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 12, :comments ["<b>And the hands of Moses grew heavy.</b> It was too hard for him to stand all day, as it says, 'Tomorrow I will stand on the top of the hill,' and it was too hard for him to raise his hand every moment. For the standing they devised: they took a stone and placed it under him, etc. And because his hands grew heavy to raise the staff of the Elohim every moment, they devised that Aaron and Hur would support him, <b>one on this side and one on that side</b>, so that the people's heart would not fail if he lowered it. And this caused that <b>his hands were emunah until the sun came</b>. Anything that stands firm in its strength and its stance, and does not weaken and is not diminished from what it was, is called emunah, as in 'I will fasten him as a peg in a place of ne'eman,' whose translation is 'in a lasting place.' And there are many more; so too here, 'his hands were emunah' — steady and standing and raising the staff of the Elohim <b>until the sun came</b>. And this caused <b>Joshua to weaken Amalek and his people</b>, for Israel would see Moses' hands raised with the staff and prevail. It is written <b>'his hand'</b> and it is written 'his hands' — rather, at first he would raise one hand and lower one hand, until both his hands began to grow heavy. Found in the midrash: <b>'his hands'</b> — spread out to heaven. <b>Emunah</b> — to invoke the merit of Abraham, of whom it says, 'and he believed in HaShem.' <b>'Until he came'</b> — to invoke the merit of Isaac, of whom it says, 'and Isaac came from the coming.' 'The sun' — to invoke the merit of Jacob, of whom it says, 'for the sun came.' <b>Emunah</b> — a plural form, just as they say for many 'omenim' (faithful ones), so for many one may say 'emunah,' as in 'the daughters of Tz'ada stepped upon the shor' (ox), and 'his eyes were kamah' (dim) — these were cities of the mu'edah. <b>Emunah</b> in the plural, like 'ke'ma' for eyes."], :book :exodus, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 17, :notes {0 "The final examples (בנות צעדה, עיניו קמה, ערי המועדה) cite unusual plural or singular forms of words that normally appear otherwise; the text as given appears slightly garbled at the end, and the translation is hyper-literal. 'Shor' and 'ke'ma' are identified from standard rabbinic grammar discussion of irregular number forms."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:58:28.420704236Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}